    "shortDefinition": "What you take truth to be: a mind-independent reality that inquiry can genuinely reach, or a human construction that happens to work.",
    "expandedDefinition": "This axis is about what knowing achieves. The realist holds there is a way the world is, independent of anyone's beliefs, and that our best methods track reality itself; disagreement is resolvable in principle because there is something to be right about. The anti-realist holds that we can never step outside our own concepts to compare beliefs with bare reality, so truth is better understood as what survives testing, argument, and use. Between them sits a divided map: plain fact in some territories, human construction in others. The axis does not measure rigor; anti-realists can be strict about evidence, they just tell a different story about what evidence achieves.",
    "commonMisconception": "That anti-realism means anything goes, or that realism means claiming certainty. The anti-realist can be exacting about method while denying the mirror picture of truth; the realist holds we are often wrong, which is only possible if there is something to be wrong about.",
