        "full": "Your answers lean anti-realist while keeping a foothold in the plain world: much of what passes for objective truth looks constructed to you, and some things still seem simply so. You likely treat social and evaluative knowledge, money, status, morality, as obviously human-made, and hold physics with a lighter version of the same suspicion. This usually reflects the judgment that construction is the rule and the open question is only how far down it goes. It tends to make you a natural genealogist, asking where a certainty came from before asking whether it is warranted. The strength of this lean is critical leverage on claims that hide their history; the cost is that the acid can be hard to stop, and the lean must keep explaining why its own standards deserve the exemption. Your identity axis often echoes this same construction question at personal scale. This is not cynicism; it is applying the history of knowledge to knowledge itself.",
