        "full": "You are undecided or divided on whether morality is discovered or made. You may treat some wrongs as plainly real while suspecting other norms are local custom. That division is defensible; where to draw the line is one of the oldest arguments in ethics. A middle score often means your moral confidence outruns your metaphysical confidence, holding firm judgments while the theory behind them stays open, which is a coherent way to live: the judgments do their work whether or not their metaphysics is settled. It tends to make you impatient with both the crusader's certainty and the debunker's shrug. The strength of this position is that it keeps moral seriousness while the theory question stays live; the cost is that hard public arguments, rights, justice across cultures, eventually ask which register you are speaking in. Your moral_authority axis shows where you locate the standard meanwhile. The middle here is the argument itself, occupied from inside.",
        "short": "Some wrongs read as plainly real, other norms as local custom, and the line is genuinely open. The strength is firm judgments without settled metaphysics; the cost is that public arguments eventually ask which register you mean."
