    "shortDefinition": "Your trust in science as an institution: expert consensus as the default, or a claim to be audited like any other interested party's.",
    "expandedDefinition": "Not the method axis and not the case-by-case axis: this one measures trust in the social institution, journals, peer review, expert consensus, funding structures and all. One pole holds that peer review, replication, and open criticism catch errors better than any individual can, so consensus is the proper starting point and updating with it is rationality, not conformity. The other holds that expert agreement is one voice among several: track records include confident reversals, methods have blind spots around meaning and lived experience, and claims arrive attached to funders and careers, so outsourcing judgment is the real risk. The middle trusts with conditions, strongest on physical questions, weaker where questions turn human.",
